WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.300
A practical ISO/IEC 27018 implementation starts with sponsorship and ownership.

2
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:21.960
Someone needs authority to define scope, resolve role questions, obtain evidence, and require changes across legal, privacy, security, engineering, operations, support, procurement, and sales.

3
00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:39.740
The organization should obtain lawful access to the current standard, define the organizational and service scope, identify cloud services processing PII, determine controller, processor, customer, provider, and subprocessor roles, and map PII flows and processing locations.

4
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.300
The next step is obligation and risk analysis.

5
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:54.280
Identify legal, regulatory, contractual, and customer obligations, then connect the work to the ISMS and Privacy Information Management System where applicable.

6
00:00:54.380 --> 00:01:05.980
Perform a gap assessment, evaluate risk, select and adapt controls, assign owners, and update contracts, procedures, architecture, and configurations.

7
00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.240
The implementation should not treat a generic checklist as sufficient.

8
00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:16.980
The selected controls need to match the service scope, processing role, customer commitments, and risk environment.

9
00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:22.620
Evidence should be collected during normal operation, not assembled as a last-minute audit exhibit.

10
00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:56.640
Useful evidence can include scope statements, data-flow diagrams, PII inventories, processing records, responsibility matrices, risk assessments, applicability and control-mapping records, contracts, data-processing terms, subprocessor registers, access reviews, privileged-access logs, encryption configurations, key-management records, deletion records, incident exercises, notification procedures, training records, internal audit results, corrective-action records, management reviews, and independent assurance reports.

11
00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.880
Evidence is not the same as assurance.

12
00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:11.580
A screenshot can show a setting at a moment in time, but assurance asks whether the control is suitably designed, operating in the stated period, scoped to the service, and supported by reliable review.

13
00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:15.200
A certificate or report can help, but it has boundaries.

14
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:31.300
Customers should evaluate the services covered, regions covered, legal entity covered, audit period, control exceptions, complementary customer controls, subservice organizations, report limitations, evidence freshness, and remediation status.

15
00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:34.380
Marketing statements need careful interpretation.

16
00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:38.720
ISO develops standards, but ISO does not certify organizations.

17
00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:45.780
Independent certification is performed by certification bodies or other assurance parties depending on the claim and assessment model.

18
00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:51.560
Guidance standards and management-system requirements standards have different conformity implications.

19
00:02:51.660 --> 00:03:00.300
A provider should identify the service, scope, period, standard edition, assessment basis, and responsible assurance party.

20
00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:05.760
It should not imply broader certification or legal compliance than the evidence supports.

21
00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:11.800
From the customer perspective, provider evaluation should be evidence-based and service-specific.

22
00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:16.460
A badge on a website may be useful as a starting signal, but it is not enough.

23
00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:28.400
The review should ask what was assessed, when, by whom, against which edition, for which services and regions, with what exceptions, and with what responsibilities left to the customer.

24
00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:37.460
Complementary customer controls are especially important because a cloud service can be well controlled by the provider and still be misconfigured by the customer.

25
00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:41.900
Implementation does not end at launch or audit.

26
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:49.520
Cloud services, subprocessors, architectures, customer requirements, and legal obligations evolve.

27
00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:57.540
Management review, monitoring, corrective action, change assessment, and periodic re-evaluation keep the program aligned with reality.

28
00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:10.600
ISO/IEC 27018 is most valuable when it becomes part of the operating rhythm: scope, assess, implement, collect evidence, review, correct, and improve.

29
00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:14.640
A useful implementation artifact is an evidence map.

30
00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:25.300
For each major guidance area, list the accountable owner, implementation reference, operational record, review frequency, customer dependency, and assurance source.

31
00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:35.000
This map helps prevent two common problems: controls that exist but produce no evidence, and evidence that exists but does not prove the claimed control.

32
00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:45.500
It also helps customers and auditors ask sharper questions because the conversation can focus on scope, currency, exceptions, and remediation rather than broad assertions.

33
00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:49.060
The strongest assurance posture is humble and precise.

34
00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:58.360
It names what was assessed, what was not assessed, what changed since the assessment period, which exceptions remain open, and which controls rely on customer action.

35
00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:01.420
That precision builds more trust than broad claims.
